############################################################
# PG Setup Code
############################################################
$lb = random(-2,-1,1);
$ub = random(1,4,1);
$m = random(2,5,1);
$b = non_zero_random(-2,2,1);
if($envir{problemSeed}==1){$lb=-1;$ub=3;$m=3;$b=-1;};
$f = Formula("$m x + $b")->reduce;
Context("Fraction");
Context()->variables->are(n=>"Real");
$sum = Fraction(($ub-$lb)*($m*($ub+$lb)+2*$b),2);
$sum = Formula("$sum");
$sum10 = $sum->eval(n=>10);
$sum100 = $sum->eval(n=>100);
$sum1000 = $sum->eval(n=>1000);
$exact = Fraction("$sum");

############################################################
# Body
############################################################

BEGIN_PGML
A definite integral is given below. As demonstrated in Examples[$NBSP]*1.3.18 and 1.3.19, do the following:
a.  Find a formula to approximate the definite integral using [`n`] subintervals and the provided rule.

a.  Evaluate the formula using [`n=10\text{,}`] [`100\text{,}`] and [`1000\text{.}`]

a.  Find the limit of the formula, as [`n\to \infty\text{,}`] to find the exact value of the definite integral.

[`\ds \int_{[$lb]}^{[$ub]} \left([$f]\right)\, dx\text{,}`] using the Midpoint Rule.

[@KeyboardInstructions('Enter the formula in terms of [`n`] here.')@]**

[_]{$sum}{30}

[@KeyboardInstructions('Evaluate the formula using [`n=10`] here.')@]**

[_]{$sum10}{10}

[@KeyboardInstructions('Evaluate the formula using [`n=100`] here.')@]**

[_]{$sum100}{10}

[@KeyboardInstructions('Evaluate the formula using [`n=1000`] here.')@]**

[_]{$sum1000}{10}

[@KeyboardInstructions('Enter the limit here.')@]**

[_]{$exact}{20}

END_PGML

